# Git Checkout

## `git checkout`

The `git checkout` command can be used to discard changes in tracked files in the working directory.

### Basic syntax

```bash
git checkout <file-name>
```

Example:

```bash
git checkout index.txt
```

---

## Working Directory → Staging Area → Local Repository

Git works with different areas:

```text
Working Directory
       |
       | git add
       ↓
Staging Area
       |
       | git commit
       ↓
Local Repository
```

When a tracked file is modified in the working directory, Git can restore its previous version using the `git checkout` command.

---

## Discarding Unstaged Changes

Suppose you have a tracked file:

```text
index.txt
```

You make some changes to it:

```text
Modified file
```

But you have not staged those changes yet.

You can discard those unstaged changes using:

```bash
git checkout index.txt
```

This restores the file to its previous version.

---

## Important Point

`git checkout` is used for **tracked files**.

It is **not applicable to untracked files**.

Example of an untracked file:

```text
newfile.txt
```

If Git has never tracked the file, `git checkout` cannot restore it because Git has no previous version stored for that file.

---

## Example

Check the status:

```bash
git status
```

Suppose:

```text
modified: index.txt
```

To discard the unstaged changes:

```bash
git checkout index.txt
```

Then check again:

```bash
git status
```

The changes made to `index.txt` will be removed.

---

## Important Warning ⚠️

Using:

```bash
git checkout <file-name>
```

can permanently discard your unstaged changes.

So make sure you really do not need those changes before running the command.

---

## Quick Summary

```text
Tracked file
     ↓
Modified in Working Directory
     ↓
Unstaged Changes
     ↓
git checkout <file-name>
     ↓
Changes are discarded
     ↓
File restored to previous version
```

### Command

```bash
git checkout <file-name>
```

### Example

```bash
git checkout index.txt
```

### Remember

- Works with tracked files
- Discards unstaged changes
- Restores the previous version of the file
- Does not work for untracked files
- Be careful because discarded changes can be lost